  "questions": [
    {
      "id": "loop-margin-design-r1-q1",
      "round": 1,
      "type": "mc",
      "prompt": "Why do control designers usually ask for a phase margin of 45–60° rather than just 'more than 0°'?",
      "options": [
        "Margins give room for model errors, delays and gain drift, and larger margins mean less overshoot and ringing in the closed-loop response",
        "Because a loop with less than 45° is always unstable",
        "Because phase margin sets the steady-state error",
        "Because it maximizes the bandwidth"
      ],
      "correct_index": 0,
      "explanation": "Any PM above 0° is stable on paper. But the real plant differs from the model: unmodeled lags, delays and gain changes eat into the margin. Small margins also mean lightly damped closed-loop poles (\\(\\zeta\\approx\\mathrm{PM}/100^\\circ\\)). 45–60° balances speed against robustness.\n• Between 0° and 45° the loop is stable, just poorly damped.\n• Steady-state error depends on the low-frequency loop gain.\n• More margin generally costs bandwidth, not the reverse."
    },
    {
      "id": "loop-margin-design-r1-q2",
      "round": 1,
      "type": "numeric",
      "prompt": "A position loop has \\(L(s)=\\dfrac{10}{s(s+3)}\\). What is its phase margin, in degrees? (Crossover is at \\(\\omega_c=2.54\\ \\mathrm{rad/s}\\).)",
