    {
      "id": "loop-margin-design-r1-q3",
      "round": 1,
      "type": "mc",
      "prompt": "A loop has a gain margin of 6 dB. What does that mean?",
      "options": [
        "The loop is unstable by 6 dB",
        "The loop gain could roughly double before the loop reaches the stability limit",
        "The output overshoots by 6 dB",
        "The loop gain can be reduced by half before it goes unstable"
      ],
      "correct_index": 1,
      "explanation": "Gain margin is how far \\(|L|\\) sits below 1 at the \\(-180^\\circ\\) frequency. 6 dB is a factor of 2, so doubling the gain would put \\(L=-1\\) there.\n• A positive margin means stable.\n• Margins describe the loop, not overshoot in dB.\n• For a typical loop, lowering the gain does not destabilize it; the margin is the allowed increase."
    },
    {
      "id": "loop-margin-design-r2-q1",
      "round": 2,
      "type": "numeric",
      "prompt": "For the same loop \\(L(s)=\\dfrac{10}{s(s+3)}\\) (PM = 49.7° at \\(\\omega_c=2.54\\ \\mathrm{rad/s}\\)), how much pure time delay can be added before the loop goes unstable, in s?",
      "answer": 0.341236,
      "unit": "s",
      "tolerance": 0.02,
      "explanation": "A delay subtracts \\(\\omega\\tau\\) of phase. Instability occurs when \\(\\omega_c\\tau=\\mathrm{PM}\\) in radians: \\(\\tau=0.868/2.54=0.341\\ \\mathrm{s}\\). Sensor sampling, communication and computation all add delay, so this is a practical budget.\n• \\(49.7/2.54=19.6\\) uses degrees.\n• Multiplying by \\(\\omega_c\\) instead of dividing gives 2.2."
    },
    {
